  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef and Chefina are ordering pizza for dinner.\n\nEach pizza has NNN slices.\n\nWhat is the minimum number of pizzas they need to order, so that both Chef and Chefina can eat an equal number of slices?\n\nChef and Chefina must eat a **positive integer** number of slices.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first and only line of input will contain a single integer NNN, the number of slices in one pizza.\n\n\nOutput Format\nPrint a single integer: the minimum number of pizzas that must be ordered so that Chef and Chefina can eat an equal number of slices.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤N≤101 \\leq N \\leq 101≤N≤10"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "6",
        "output": "1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nOne pizza has 666 slices. Chef and Chefina can eat 333 each."
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3",
        "output": "2",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nOne pizza is not enough for Chef and Chefina to eat an equal number of slices.\nInstead, they must order two pizzas, and then eat 333 slices each."
      }
    ]
  },
